position uncertainty in strap-down inertial navigation systems [4]. During locomotion of humanoid robots and humans, the phase when the foot is in contact with the ground can be regarded as a zero-velocity condition [3]. The zero-velocity information from foot contact can be treated as a pseudomeasurement to constrain and correct velocity estimation errors in inertial navigation systems [31]. Therefore, by mounting IMUs on the feet of robots, the zero-velocity information can be effectively utilized to enhance the accuracy of inertial navigation. Hartley et al. achieved remarkable results using a fusion strategy based on IMU and contact sensing [10]. However, their method relies on dedicated contact sensors. Yang et al. mounted multiple IMUs on the foot and integrated their measurements within an extended Kalman filter (EKF) framework to realize low-cost proprioceptive odometry [25]. Nevertheless, the approach employs a relatively basic EKF and does not account for IMU bias. The integration of zero-velocity updates within EKF has proven effective in significantly reducing drift in foot-mounted inertial navigation systems [5], [6]. However, conventional EKF implementations rely on Jacobian-based linearization in Euclidean space, which can suffer from inconsistency and degraded performance when confronted with large estimation errors or strongly nonlinear system dynamics. To address the limitations, invariant extended Kalman filter (InEKF) [8] performs state estimation directly on matrix Lie groups, preserving the underlying geometric structure of the system. This formulation yields trajectory-independent error dynamics under the group-affine condition, leading to improved consistency, more straightforward tuning, and provable stability in deterministic settings [18]. Furthermore, the observability properties of InEKF with stationary pseudo-measurements have been analytically characterized [21]. InEKF has been successfully applied to robotic state estimation, encompassing bipedal locomotion [10], [19], [26] and a wide range of navigation and localization tasks [11], [20], [23]. The applications demonstrate the effectiveness of InEKF as an alternative to conventional
